#{[17592186180079 :question/worked-solution "The remaining fraction is $6.25/200=1/32=(1/2)^5$. Five half-lives have elapsed, so $t=5(15.2)=76\\,\\mathrm{s}$."] [17592186180080 :question/worked-solution "At the half-life, $e^{-0.001k}=1/2$. Therefore $k=\\ln2/0.001=693.147\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, which is $693$ to three significant figures."] [17592186180169 :question/worked-solution "The remaining fraction satisfies $0.70=2^{-t/12.3}$. Therefore $t=12.3\\ln(0.70)/\\ln(0.5)=6.3292\\ldots$ years, or $6.3$ years to one decimal place."] [17592186181456 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186181460] [17592186181462 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186181467] [17592186181468 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186181471] [17592186181502 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186181507] [17592186183042 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186183043] [17592186183048 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186183050] [17592186184548 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 12 x^{3} + 5 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=3x^{4} + \\frac{5x^{2}}{2} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{4} + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2}+C\\right)=12 x^{3} + 5 x.\n$$"] [17592186184549 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt{x} - 3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}{3} - 3x + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2 x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 3 x+C\\right)=\\sqrt{x} - 3.\n$$"] [17592186184550 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - 3 x + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{3x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{1}{x} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{1}{x}+C\\right)=- 3 x + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186184563 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - \\sqrt{x} + x^{2} + 2\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{3} - 2\\sqrt{x^{3}} + 6x}{3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{2 x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2 x+C\\right)=- \\sqrt{x} + x^{2} + 2.\n$$"] [17592186184738 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x^{\\frac{5}{2}} + \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{5}{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{x^{7}}}{7} - \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x^{3}}} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2 x^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{7} - \\frac{2}{3 x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}+C\\right)=x^{\\frac{5}{2}} + \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{5}{2}}}.\n$$"] [17592186184739 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt[5]{x} + 4 x^{7} - \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{13}{7}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{8}}{2} + \\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{x^{6}}}{6} + \\frac{7}{6\\sqrt[7]{x^{6}}} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 x^{\\frac{6}{5}}}{6} + \\frac{x^{8}}{2} + \\frac{7}{6 x^{\\frac{6}{7}}}+C\\right)=\\sqrt[5]{x} + 4 x^{7} - \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{13}{7}}}.\n$$"] [17592186228333 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228337] [17592186228345 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228346] [17592186228567 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186228571] [17592186229692 :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=0$ into the stated model $Q(t)=\\alpha+\\beta e^{-kt}$. Since $e^0=1$ and the initial salt mass is $6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $\\alpha+\\beta=Q(0)=6$."] [17592186229694 :question/worked-solution "The volume is $V(t)=120+(9-6)t=120+3t$. Overflow begins when $V=210$, so $t=30\\,\\mathrm{min}$. Mass balance gives $Q′=4.5-6Q/(120+3t)$. Its solution satisfying $Q(0)=30$ is $Q=\\frac12(120+3t)-30(120/(120+3t))^2$. At $t=30$, $Q=95.204\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{lb}$, hence $95.2\\,\\mathrm{lb}$."] [17592186229725 :question/worked-solution "The volume stays $900\\,\\mathrm{L}$. The salt inflow is $0.02(30)=0.6\\,\\mathrm{kg/min}$ and outflow is $30Q/900=Q/30$. Thus $Q′+Q/30=0.6$. With $Q(0)=10$, $Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}$. Consequently $Q(6)=11.4501\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, or $11.45\\,\\mathrm{kg}$."] [17592186235391 :question/problem "$$\n\\int y^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\ln({{field-1}})+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186235444 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186238339 :answer/value "-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186238465 :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x"]}
